<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cerball mac Dúnlainge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 888</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Carroll, King of Osraige</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cerball mac Dúnlainge was king of Osraige in Ireland from about 842 until his death in 888. By fighting and allying with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leaders, and by exploiting rivalries between the kings of Munster and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, he made his small kingdom a major power. Icelandic sagas remember him as Kjarval, an ancestor of many Icelandic settler families.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cerball succeeded his father, Dúnlang (or Dúngal) mac Fergaile, as king of Osraige in 842. The kingdom of the Osraige, the Deer people, covered perhaps 2,000 square kilometres astride the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-barrow">River Barrow</a>, far larger than an average túath, and lay at the eastern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a> kingdom of Munster, bordering Leinster. The High King, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mael-sechnaill-mac-maele-ruanaid">Máel Sechnaill mac Máele Ruanaid</a>, was married to Cerball's sister Land, and Cerball in turn married a daughter of Máel Sechnaill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He first appears in 846, when his army besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders in northern Osraige for a fortnight, and in 847 he defeated a Viking force led by one Hákon. The death in 847 of Fedelmid mac Crimthainn, King of Munster, who was followed by weak kings, left him in a strong position. In 855 he slaughtered a Viking force under Rodolb, was captured by other Vikings and escaped, and then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> under Horm in inflicting a heavy defeat on the Norse.</p><p>He later allied with the Norse leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amlaib-conung">Amlaíb Conung</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imar">Ímar</a>, campaigning with Ímar in Leinster in 858 and raiding Máel Sechnaill's lands in Mide the following year, though each time he had to submit to the high king again. After Máel Sechnaill died in 862, his successor Áed Finnliath married Land, and in 870 Cerball joined Áed's army in Leinster. Much of this comes from the Fragmentary Annals of Ireland, which were meant to magnify his deeds and break off in the early 870s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cerball died in 888 and was succeeded by his brother Riagan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annales-cambriae">Annales Cambriae</a> note his death under 887; he is the only Irish king they mention who was not an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> high king. He was the male-line ancestor of the later kings of Osraige and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-giolla-phadraig">Mac Giolla Phádraig</a> clan, and historians regard him as a patron of building and of Osraige's tradition of carved high crosses.</p><p>The Icelandic Landnámabók names him, as Kjarvalur, alongside rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>, and makes him the maternal grandfather of the settler Helgi the Lean. Historians treat these genealogies as unreliable; one suggestion is that the Fragmentary Annals were popular among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norse-gaels">Norse-Gaels</a> of 11th-century Ireland, who passed his story on to the Icelanders.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cerball mac Dúnlainge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Cerball mac Dúnlainge</b>, who died in 888, made Osraige a major power by fighting and allying with Viking leaders.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-osraige">Kingdom of Osraige</a></li></ul></section>
